  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
<para>Here is a graph.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Graph of line, origin O. Horizontal axis 0 to 7, by 1’s, labeled x. Vertical from 0 to 150, by 50’s, labeled y. Starts at 0 comma 120, appears to pass through 1 point 5 comma 125 and 7 comma 150.   "><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/bad0b52014f58b56c36d99212daa7f56596f8dba"/></media></figure></para>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Which equation do you think the graph represents? Use the graph to support your reasoning. </para>
    <para> \(y=120+(3.7) \cdot x\) </para>
      <para> \(y=120 \cdot (1.03)^x\) </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <list>
      All answers are acceptable if backed up by a reasonable explanation. Compare your answer:
      <list>
        <item>The graph is for \(y=120+3.7x\) because it appears linear. On the graph, when \(x=7\), the value of \(y\) appears to be a little less than 150. When \(x=7\), \(120+(3.7) \cdot 7=145.9\), so the slope of the line could be 3.7.</item>
        <item>The graph is for \(y=120 \cdot (1.03)^x\). Multiplying 100 by 1.03 seven times, or \(100 \cdot (1.03)^7\), gives approximately 147.6, which more or less concurs with what is on the graph.</item>
        <item>It is hard to tell. I tried finding the \(y\)-coordinate values for different values of  \(x\) using both equations, and they more or less agree with what is on the graph.</item>
      </list>
    </list>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What information might help you decide more easily whether the graph represents a linear or an exponential function? </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Your answers may vary, but here are some examples: </para>
    <list>
      <item>Exact coordinates of points on the graph with whole-number \(x\)-values </item>
      <item>A larger graphing window showing larger domain values so that the behavior of the function can be more visible </item>
    </list>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
